# Folder structure

```
src/
  app/              App.tsx, main.tsx, composition root (wires concrete services)
  content/          heroContent.ts — all user-facing copy and nav data, typed + readonly
  domain/signup/    EmailAddress.ts, SignupService.ts (contract), signupTypes.ts
  infrastructure/
    graphql/        graphqlRequest.ts, GraphQLSignupService.ts
    mock/           MockSignupService.ts
  features/
    navigation/     NavBar.tsx, DesktopNav.tsx, MobileMenu.tsx, MenuOverlay.tsx
    hero/           Hero.tsx, CalloutPill.tsx, HeroIllustration.tsx
    signup/         SignupForm.tsx, useSignupForm.ts
  ui/               Button.tsx, Text.tsx, TextInput.tsx, Container.tsx, VisuallyHidden.tsx
    icons/          LogoMark.tsx, MenuIcon.tsx, CloseIcon.tsx, ChevronRightIcon.tsx
  shared/
    hooks/          useDisclosure, useEscapeKey, useFocusTrap, useScrollLock, useMediaQuery
    lib/            result.ts, assertNever.ts, cn.ts
  styles/           index.css (Tailwind import + @theme tokens + @font-face)
  assets/           raw exported SVGs (source material)
```

# Dependency direction (enforce with `no-restricted-imports` per folder)

- `domain/` imports nothing from React, `features/`, `ui/`, or `infrastructure/`. Pure TypeScript.
- `infrastructure/` implements `domain/` contracts. It never imports React or UI.
- `features/` depend on `domain/` contracts, `ui/`, `shared/` and `content/` — never on concrete `infrastructure/` classes.
- `ui/` knows nothing about features or domain. Pure presentational primitives.
- `app/` is the only place that instantiates concrete services and injects them (through a `SignupServiceProvider` context or props).

# Lightweight DDD — scope is deliberate

- The only real domain is the email signup. Model it with:
  - `EmailAddress` value object: class with a private constructor, `static create(raw: string): Result<EmailAddress, EmailValidationError>`, a `value` getter and `equals()`. Invalid instances cannot exist.
  - `SignupService` interface: the port. `MockSignupService` and `GraphQLSignupService` are adapters.
- Do NOT add repositories, aggregates, domain events, use-case classes, DI containers, or extra layers. A landing page padded with ceremony counts against the author.
- OOP is limited to the value object and the service adapters. Components and hooks are functions.

# Reusability

- Extract a component when it is used twice or when the Figma Symbols page defines it: Nav link, Nav button link, Menu trigger, Mobile menu link, Mobile menu close button, Submit button, Text input.
- No speculative abstraction, no "just in case" props.
- All copy and nav items come from `content/heroContent.ts` and are passed in as props. Components never hard-code user-facing strings.

# Files

- One component per file, named export, file name matches the export.
- Keep files short (aim for under 150 lines); split by responsibility when they grow.
- Colocate tests next to the file they test.
